From 2e89b8969a1fd1966a403dd8aa5ea35f8d0d719e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:50:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/24] Hand the device lock over in turn, and record each vCPU
 wait
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The lock-stall census put a 7.8 s and a 6.2 s vCPU wait in report
windows whose longest hold was 71 ms and 116 ms, which reads as the
drain worker taking the lock again and again past a blocked vCPU. Each
was one hold. The worker's release wrote the window's line before the
vCPU it released had counted its wait, so the wait landed a line after
the hold that caused it: drain=…/8266534 (draw_stall 8197256, engine
worker_hold_max 8197265) a line before the 7803935 us wait, and
drain=…/7677605 a line before the 6227906 us one. In neither boot was a
wait longer than the longest hold of its own or the preceding window.

Line them up. A hold is counted before its release, a vCPU's wait before
it deregisters, and while a vCPU is registered no release writes the
window's line, for one more window at most.

Record each wait. A vCPU registers before it blocks and deregisters, by
a guard that also runs on unwind, once it has the lock. While it is
registered, every acquisition and release goes into its record: how many
times the drain worker took the lock first, how many times anyone else
did, how much of the wait the lock was held, and the longest hold that
ended in it. What is left of the wait is the lock free and the vCPU not
running, which tells a descheduled vCPU from a starved one. A wait past
50 ms writes its record as a device_lock_wait line naming the guest CPU,
read from the QEMU thread name. device_lock_hold adds a wait
distribution per vCPU, the drain worker's own contended waits
(drain_wait), and how many of its releases found a vCPU waiting
(drain_handoffs).

And hand the lock over in the order threads blocked: every release is
unlock_fair, so a parked thread is given the lock instead of racing a
worker that comes straight back for it, a try_lock caller cannot take it
mid-handover, and vCPUs that never stop arriving cannot keep the worker
out. A plain parking_lot release hands over only when its fairness timer
has run out, up to a millisecond apart. With a worker re-taking the lock
after each 100 us hold, a blocked vCPU was overtaken at most once in 200
waits, against 8-9 times with a plain release; that still kept its wait
near a millisecond, which is also why repeated re-taking cannot explain a
wait of seconds. With two vCPUs taking the lock back to back, the worker
waited at most 1.9 ms (3-6 ms plain) and each vCPU was overtaken by the
other at most once (15-22 times plain).

This shortens no hold. A vCPU behind an 8 s pipeline compile still waits
8 s, holding the BQL. Nothing waits or backs off that did not before, no
lock is taken that was not, and each holder does the same work in the
same order. The record's own mutex is a leaf, taken with the device lock
held or with nothing held.

Also move window_publish's doc comment back onto window_publish.

